exec(open('/home/claude/work/devim/dmc.py').read())
C=3
def MAN(x,y,s=1): return f'<g transform="translate({x} {y}) scale({s})">'+ASURA(0,0,1,'#7a2a2a')+SWORD(16,-6,30,.6)+'</g>'
def ELEPHANT(x,y,s=1): return MOUNT('elephant',x,y,s)
V=[
VP(C,'2–3','nihanyamānaṁ tatsainyamavalokya mahāsuraḥ | / senānīścikṣuraḥ kopādyayau yoddhumathāmbikām || 2|| / sa devīṁ śaravarṣeṇa vavarṣa samare’suraḥ | / yathā merugireḥ śṛṅgaṁ toyavarṣeṇa toyadaḥ || 3||',
 'CIKṢURA ATTACKS',
 sc('An asura general in a chariot pouring arrows on the Goddess like a rain cloud pouring water on a mountain peak',BATTLE+'<path d="M140 130 L180 40 L220 130z" fill="#dfe9f6" stroke="#111"/>'+'<ellipse cx="170" cy="30" rx="40" ry="16" fill="#777" stroke="#111"/>'+''.join(f'<path d="M{150+k*8} 46 l-3 14" stroke="#2a6ab0" stroke-width="2"/>' for k in range(6))+CHARIOT(50,136,.9)+ASURA(50,100,.6)+ARROWS(70,80,4)+DEVI(130,40,.4,False)),
 'Seeing his army being slaughtered, the great asura Cikṣura, the general, went in anger to fight Ambikā. That asura rained arrows on the Devī in battle, as a cloud rains water on the peak of Mount Meru.',
 [('nihanyamānam','<b>being slaughtered</b>'),('senānī','<b>general</b>'),('śara-varṣa','<b>a rain of arrows</b>'),('meru-gireḥ śṛṅgam','<b>the peak of Mount Meru</b>'),('toyada','<b>a rain cloud</b>, “water-giver”')],
 '<b>Rain on a mountain.</b> The simile says it all: a downpour does not hurt a mountain peak. Chapter 3 is the duel with Mahiṣa himself, and it starts with his generals throwing everything at her, uselessly.',
 'Arrows on her are <b>rain on a mountain</b>.'),
VP(C,'8–10','tasyāḥ khaḍgo bhujaṁ prāpya paphāla nṛpanandana | / tato jagrāha śūlaṁ sa kopādaruṇalocanaḥ || 8|| / cikṣepa ca tatastattu bhadrakālyāṁ mahāsuraḥ | / jājvalyamānaṁ tejobhī ravibimbamivāmbarāt || 9|| / dṛṣṭvā tadāpatacchūlaṁ devī śūlamamuñcata | / tena tacchatadhā nītaṁ śūlaṁ sa ca mahāsuraḥ || 10||',
 'THE SWORD SHATTERS',
 sc('An asura’s sword breaking into pieces against the Goddess’s arm; a flaming spear flying at her, met by her own trident',BATTLE+DEVI(150,10,.7)+ASURA(40,150,.9)+''.join(f'<path d="M{96+k*6} {60+k*5} l6 -4" stroke="#999" stroke-width="3"/>' for k in range(4))+'<circle cx="90" cy="40" r="12" fill="#ff8c1a" stroke="#c0392b" stroke-width="3"/>'+TRIDENT(112,70,.6)),
 'His sword, reaching her arm, shattered, O prince! Then he seized a spear, his eyes red with anger, and the great asura hurled it at Bhadrakālī, blazing with radiance like the sun’s disc falling from the sky. Seeing that spear falling, the Devī let fly her own spear, and by it his spear was broken into a hundred pieces, and the great asura too.',
 [('paphāla','<b>shattered</b>'),('nṛpa-nandana','<b>O prince!</b>: Medhas speaking to King Suratha'),('aruṇa-locana','<b>red-eyed</b>'),('bhadrakālī','<b>the auspicious Kālī</b>'),('śatadhā','<b>into a hundred pieces</b>')],
 '<b>“O prince!”</b> Remember who is telling this: the sage Medhas, to the king who lost everything (Part 5). He keeps turning to him. These battles are being told to a man who needs to learn what the Goddess can do.',
 'The sword breaks <b>on her arm</b>.'),
VP(C,'14–16','tataḥ siṁhaḥ samutpatya gajakumbhāntare sthitaḥ | / bāhuyuddhena yuyudhe tenoccaistridaśāriṇā || 14|| / yudhyamānau tatastau tu tasmānnāgānmahīṁ gatau | / yuyudhāte’tisaṁrabdhau prahārairatidāruṇaiḥ || 15|| / tato vegāt khamutpatya nipatya ca mṛgāriṇā | / karaprahāreṇa śiraścāmarasya pṛthak kṛtam || 16||',
 'THE LION FIGHTS',
 sc('The Goddess’s lion leaping onto the head of an elephant ridden by the asura Camara and wrestling him',BATTLE+ELEPHANT(100,120,1.3)+LION(110,74,.8)+ASURA(140,90,.7)),
 'Then the lion sprang up and, standing between the elephant’s temples, fought hand to hand with that enemy of the gods, Cāmara. Fighting, the two fell from the elephant to the ground and fought on in fury with terrible blows. Then the lion leapt swiftly into the sky, came down, and with a blow of his paw struck Cāmara’s head from his body.',
 [('siṃha','<b>the lion</b>'),('gaja-kumbha','<b>the elephant’s temples</b>'),('bāhu-yuddha','<b>wrestling</b>, arm-to-arm fight'),('mṛga-ari','<b>enemy of beasts</b>: the lion'),('kara-prahāra','<b>a blow of the paw</b>')],
 '<b>The vāhana is a warrior.</b> Her lion is not decoration. He leaps, wrestles and kills a general by himself. In temple images the lion often bites a demon’s leg: this is where that comes from.',
 'Her lion <b>fights beside her</b>.'),
VP(C,'21–23','evaṁ saṅkṣīyamāṇe tu svasainye mahiṣāsuraḥ | / māhiṣeṇa svarūpeṇa trāsayāmāsa tān gaṇān || 21|| / kāṁścittuṇḍaprahāreṇa khurakṣepaistathāparān | / lāṅgūlatāḍitāṁścānyān śṛṅgābhyāṁ ca vidāritān || 22|| / vegena kāṁścidaparānnādena bhramaṇena ca | / niḥśvāsapavanenānyānpātayāmāsa bhūtale || 23||',
 'MAHIṢA ENTERS',
 sc('A huge buffalo goring, kicking and lashing its tail at the Goddess’s troops, who fly in every direction',BATTLE+BUFFALO(110,110,1.4)+''.join(GOD(x,y,.35,'#ff8c1a','#c98b5a',False) for x,y in [(30,60),(50,140),(190,60),(200,140),(160,30)])+'<path d="M170 100 q20 -10 30 6" fill="none" stroke="#9fd3f0" stroke-width="3"/>'),
 'As his army was being destroyed, Mahiṣāsura, in his own buffalo form, terrified her troops. Some he struck with his muzzle, others with kicks of his hooves, others he lashed with his tail, and others he tore with his horns. Some he felled with his speed, others with his bellowing and wheeling about, and others with the wind of his breath.',
 [('māhiṣa svarūpa','<b>his own buffalo form</b>'),('tuṇḍa','<b>muzzle</b>'),('khura','<b>hoof</b>'),('lāṅgūla','<b>tail</b>'),('śṛṅga','<b>horn</b>'),('niḥśvāsa-pavana','<b>the wind of his breath</b>')],
 '<b>The real form.</b> “His own form” (svarūpa) is the buffalo. In Indian thought the buffalo stands for <i>tamas</i>: heavy, dull, blind force. Every part of him becomes a weapon: horn, hoof, tail, even breath.',
 'Mahiṣa is <b>blind brute force</b>.'),
VP(C,'25–27','so’pi kopānmahāvīryaḥ khurakṣuṇṇamahītalaḥ | / śṛṅgābhyāṁ parvatānuccāṁścikṣepa ca nanāda ca || 25|| / vegabhramaṇavikṣuṇṇā mahī tasya vyaśīryata | / lāṅgūlenāhataścābdhiḥ plāvayāmāsa sarvataḥ || 26|| / dhutaśṛṅgavibhinnāśca khaṇḍaṁ khaṇḍaṁ yayurghanāḥ | / śvāsānilāstāḥ śataśo nipeturnabhaso’calāḥ || 27||',
 'THE WORLD SHAKES',
 sc('The buffalo hurling mountains with its horns, its tail whipping the ocean into a flood, clouds torn apart above',HIMA+'<path d="M0 130 q27 -24 55 0 t55 0 t55 0 t55 0 V160 H0z" fill="#2a6ab0" stroke="#111"/>'+BUFFALO(110,112,1.2)+'<path d="M150 50 l20 -30 l20 30z" fill="#9a8a7a" stroke="#111"/>'+'<ellipse cx="50" cy="26" rx="26" ry="10" fill="#bbb" stroke="#111"/><ellipse cx="80" cy="30" rx="16" ry="8" fill="#bbb" stroke="#111"/>'),
 'He too, mighty, in anger, pounding the earth with his hooves, tossed lofty mountains with his horns and bellowed. Crushed by his speed and wheeling, the earth broke apart; struck by his tail, the ocean flooded everywhere. Torn by his tossing horns, the clouds went to pieces; blown by the wind of his breath, mountains fell from the sky in hundreds.',
 [('khura-kṣuṇṇa','<b>pounded by hooves</b>'),('vyaśīryata','<b>broke apart</b>'),('abdhi','<b>the ocean</b>'),('ghanāḥ','<b>clouds</b>'),('acalāḥ','<b>mountains</b>, “the unmoving”')],
 '<b>Ego out of control.</b> Mahiṣa’s rage damages the whole world: earth, ocean, sky. This is what unchecked tamas does. The verses build tension before the Goddess answers.',
 'His rage <b>wrecks the world</b>.'),
VP(C,'29–30','sā kṣiptvā tasya vai pāśaṁ taṁ babandha mahāsuram | / tatyāja māhiṣaṁ rūpaṁ so’pi baddho mahāmṛdhe || 29|| / tataḥ siṁho’bhavatsadyo yāvattasyāmbikā śiraḥ | / chinatti tāvat puruṣaḥ khaḍgapāṇiradṛśyata || 30||',
 'BUFFALO, LION, MAN',
 sc('Three stages in a row: the buffalo caught in a noose, a lion appearing where it stood, and then a man with a sword',BATTLE+BUFFALO(40,100,.6)+'<circle cx="40" cy="90" r="22" fill="none" stroke="#2a6ab0" stroke-width="3"/>'+'<path d="M74 90 h14" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/>'+LION(116,100,.6)+'<path d="M146 90 h14" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/>'+MAN(188,120,.8)),
 'She threw her noose over him and bound the great asura. Bound in the great battle, he gave up his buffalo form. At once he became a lion; and just as Ambikā was cutting off its head, a man appeared with a sword in his hand.',
 [('pāśa','<b>noose</b>: Varuṇa’s gift'),('babandha','<b>bound</b>'),('tatyāja','<b>gave up</b>'),('puruṣaḥ khaḍga-pāṇiḥ','<b>a man, sword in hand</b>')],
 '<b>The shape-shifter.</b> Tied down in one form, Mahiṣa slips into another. Ego does this: catch it as anger and it becomes pride; catch it as pride and it becomes cleverness. The chase that follows is famous.',
 'Caught in one form, <b>ego slips into another</b>.'),
VP(C,'31–32','tata evāśu puruṣaṁ devī ciccheda sāyakaiḥ | / taṁ khaḍgacarmaṇā sārdhaṁ tataḥ so’bhūnmahāgajaḥ || 31|| / kareṇa ca mahāsiṁhaṁ taṁ cakarṣa jagarja ca | / karṣatastu karaṁ devī khaḍgena nirakṛntata || 32||',
 'MAN, THEN ELEPHANT',
 sc('An elephant dragging the lion with its trunk while the Goddess swings her sword at the trunk',BATTLE+ELEPHANT(70,110,1.2)+LION(150,120,.7)+DEVI(160,6,.55,False)+SWORD(120,90,-40,.9)),
 'At once the Devī cut down the man with her arrows, together with his sword and shield. Then he became a great elephant. With his trunk he dragged her great lion and trumpeted; but as he dragged, the Devī cut off his trunk with her sword.',
 [('sāyaka','<b>arrow</b>'),('khaḍga-carman','<b>sword and shield</b>'),('mahā-gaja','<b>a great elephant</b>'),('kara','<b>trunk</b>'),('nirakṛntata','<b>cut off</b>')],
 '<b>Four forms in four verses.</b> Buffalo, lion, man, elephant. Each time, she meets the new form at once. She does not chase the shapes; she simply answers each one.',
 'Whatever form it takes, <b>she answers it</b>.'),
VP(C,'33–34','tato mahāsuro bhūyo māhiṣaṁ vapurāsthitaḥ | / tathaiva kṣobhayāmāsa trailokyaṁ sacarācaram || 33|| / tataḥ kruddhā jaganmātā caṇḍikā pānamuttamam | / papau punaḥ punaścaiva jahāsāruṇalocanā || 34||',
 'SHE DRINKS AND LAUGHS',
 sc('The Goddess raising Kubera’s cup and laughing, her eyes red, while the buffalo storms below',GLOW(110,40,50,'#c0392b',.25)+DEVI(110,4,.8)+'<path d="M140 30 h18 l-4 16 h-10z" fill="#e0a800" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/>'+BUFFALO(60,140,.6)+T(110,152,'HA HA!',12,'#c0392b')),
 'Then the great asura took up his buffalo body again and, as before, shook the three worlds with all that moves and does not move. Then Caṇḍikā, Mother of the world, angered, drank the supreme drink again and again, and laughed, her eyes red.',
 [('bhūyaḥ','<b>again</b>'),('sa-cara-acaram','<b>with moving and unmoving beings</b>'),('jagat-mātā','<b>Mother of the world</b>'),('pānam uttamam','<b>the supreme drink</b>'),('jahāsa','<b>laughed</b>')],
 '<b>Kubera’s cup.</b> The cup “never empty” given in chapter 2 is used now. Commentators read the drink as divine bliss or intoxication with power, not ordinary wine. She is so sure of victory that she pauses to drink.',
 'In the thick of war she <b>pauses, drinks and laughs</b>.'),
VP(C,'38','garja garja kṣaṇaṁ mūḍha madhu yāvatpibāmyaham | / mayā tvayi hate’traiva garjiṣyantyāśu devatāḥ || 38||',
 '“ROAR, FOOL, ROAR!”',
 sc('The Goddess, cup in hand, speaking calmly to a bellowing buffalo; her speech bubble says roar while I drink',BATTLE+DEVI(150,6,.75)+BUFFALO(56,120,.9)+SPEECH(90,30,'GARJA, GARJA!',96,11)),
 'The Devī said: “Roar, roar, fool, for a moment, while I drink this honey. When you are slain by me, right here, the gods will soon be roaring.”',
 [('garja','<b>roar!</b>'),('kṣaṇam','<b>for a moment</b>'),('mūḍha','<b>fool</b>, deluded one'),('madhu','<b>honey</b>, sweet drink'),('garjiṣyanti devatāḥ','<b>the gods will roar</b>')],
 '<b>Her only words in the chapter.</b> This is one of the most quoted lines of the whole text. Mahiṣa’s roar is noise that will last “a moment”. Then the roar will change sides: the gods will be the ones shouting, in joy.',
 '“Roar a moment. <b>Then the gods will roar.</b>”'),
VP(C,'40–41','evamuktvā samutpatya sārūḍhā taṁ mahāsuram | / pādenākramya kaṇṭhe ca śūlenainamatāḍayat || 40|| / tataḥ so’pi padākrāntastayā nijamukhāttadā | / ardhaniṣkrānta evāsīddevyā vīryeṇa saṁvṛtaḥ || 41||',
 'HER FOOT ON HIS NECK',
 sc('The Goddess leaping onto the buffalo, pressing its neck with her foot and striking with her trident, a man half emerging from its mouth',BATTLE+BUFFALO(100,120,1.3)+DEVI(90,-10,.75)+TRIDENT(140,80,.8)+ASURA(160,110,.6)),
 'Saying this, she leapt up and mounted the great asura, pressed his neck with her foot, and struck him with her trident. Then he, pressed under her foot, came half out of his own mouth, held fast by the Devī’s power.',
 [('samutpatya','<b>leaping up</b>'),('pādena ākramya kaṇṭhe','<b>pressing his neck with her foot</b>'),('śūla','<b>trident</b>'),('ardha-niṣkrāntaḥ','<b>half come out</b>'),('saṃvṛtaḥ','<b>held fast</b>')],
 '<b>The famous image.</b> This is Mahiṣāsuramardinī: her foot on the buffalo, trident in its neck, the demon emerging in human form from the carcass. From Māmallapuram to every Durgā Pūjā pandal, this is the moment carved and painted.',
 'This is <b>Mahiṣāsuramardinī</b>.'),
VP(C,'42–44','ardhaniṣkrānta evāsau yudhyamāno mahāsuraḥ | / tayā mahāsinā devyā śiraśchittvā nipātitaḥ || 42|| / tato hāhākṛtaṁ sarvaṁ daityasainyaṁ nanāśa tat | / praharṣaṁ ca paraṁ jagmuḥ sakalā devatāgaṇāḥ || 43|| / tuṣṭuvustāṁ surā devīṁ sahadivyairmaharṣibhiḥ | / jagurgandharvapatayo nanṛtuścāpsarogaṇāḥ || 44||',
 'MAHIṢA FALLS',
 sc('The Goddess standing in victory over the fallen buffalo, gods cheering, celestial musicians singing and dancers dancing',FLOWERS(10)+DEVI(110,0,.75)+LION(110,140,.6)+GODS(150,.4,2,24,30)+GODS(150,.4,2,170,30)+'<path d="M40 40 q10 -10 20 0" stroke="#111" fill="none"/>'),
 'Still fighting, half come out, the great asura was felled by the Devī, who cut off his head with her great sword. Then the whole asura army, crying “alas!”, perished, and all the hosts of gods went into supreme joy. The gods with the heavenly great sages praised the Devī; the lords of the Gandharvas sang and the Apsarases danced.',
 [('mahā-asinā','<b>with a great sword</b>'),('hā-hā-kṛtam','<b>crying “alas!”</b>'),('praharṣa','<b>great joy</b>'),('gandharva','<b>celestial musicians</b>'),('apsaras','<b>celestial dancers</b>')],
 '<b>Vijayā Daśamī.</b> In the popular tradition, this victory is what Navarātri ends on: the tenth day, Vijayā Daśamī, “the victorious tenth”. The asuras cry “hā hā”; heaven sings and dances.',
 'Mahiṣa falls; <b>heaven sings and dances</b>.'),
]
cover=('<svg width="380" height="268" viewBox="0 0 380 268" role="img" aria-label="The Goddess with her foot on the buffalo demon, trident in its neck, a man emerging from its mouth, her lion beside her">'
 '<rect width="380" height="268" fill="#3a1414" rx="8"/>'+GLOW(190,90,100,'#ffd60a',.25)+BUFFALO(190,214,2.0)+DEVI(170,10,1.05)+TRIDENT(250,160,1.2)+ASURA(290,200,.9)+LION(70,236,.8)+'</svg>')
st1=storypage('generals','THE GENERALS FALL','Ślokas 3.1–20',
 panels('Three panels: arrows raining on the Goddess; the lion on the elephant; the Goddess striking down generals with mace, trident and sword',[
  ('#ffe3c4','CIKṢURA',CHARIOT(40,150,.7)+ARROWS(60,100,4)+DEVI(110,20,.4,False)),
  ('#fff3b0','CĀMARA',ELEPHANT(72,140,.9)+LION(72,104,.6)),
  ('#fde4e4','ONE BY ONE',DEVI(72,0,.6)+ASURA(30,170,.5)+ASURA(110,170,.5,'#3a2a6a'))]),
 ['<b>Cikṣura.</b> Mahiṣa’s general rains arrows on her. She cuts down his horses, charioteer, bow and banner, and when his sword shatters on her arm, her spear destroys him.',
  '<b>Cāmara</b> comes on an elephant. Her lion leaps onto the elephant and tears his head off.',
  '<b>The rest.</b> Udagra and Karāla, Uddhata, Bāṣkala, Tāmra and Andhaka, Ugrāsya, Ugravīrya and Mahāhanu, Biḍāla, Durdhara and Durmukha: all are killed.',
  '<b>Three eyes, one trident.</b> “The three-eyed Supreme Goddess” kills several with her trident; others fall to mace, sword and arrows.',
  '<b>Mahiṣa alone.</b> With his generals gone, the buffalo demon himself comes forward.'],
 ("So many names! Do they matter?","Some readers see them as faces of the ego: Ugrāsya 'fierce-mouthed', Durmukha 'foul-mouthed', Durdhara 'hard to hold'. Whatever their names, every one falls before Mahiṣa does."),
 'Every general falls <b>before Mahiṣa does</b>.')
st2=storypage('shapes','THE SHAPE-SHIFTER','Ślokas 3.21–36',
 panels('Three panels: the buffalo goring troops; the buffalo becoming a lion, a man and an elephant; the Goddess drinking from her cup and laughing',[
  ('#ffe3c4','THE BUFFALO',BUFFALO(72,120,1.0)),
  ('#dfe9f6','LION · MAN · ELEPHANT',LION(40,80,.5)+MAN(100,100,.6)+ELEPHANT(72,160,.6)),
  ('#fde4e4','SHE DRINKS',DEVI(72,10,.6)+'<path d="M100 40 h14 l-3 12 h-8z" fill="#e0a800" stroke="#111"/>')]),
 ['<b>The buffalo.</b> Mahiṣa attacks her troops in his buffalo form, then rushes at her lion. The earth breaks, the ocean floods, mountains fall from the sky.',
  '<b>The noose.</b> She binds him. He drops the buffalo form and becomes a lion.',
  '<b>A man, an elephant.</b> She beheads the lion: a man with a sword appears. She cuts down the man: a great elephant appears and drags her lion. She cuts off its trunk.',
  '<b>Buffalo again.</b> He returns to his buffalo body and shakes the three worlds.',
  '<b>The pause.</b> Caṇḍikā drinks again and again, laughs with red eyes, and grinds his hurled mountains to dust with her arrows.'],
 ("Why doesn't she just finish him while he's caught?","The story is showing how slippery he is. Then it shows her calm. She is not chasing; she is waiting, cup in hand, for the moment."),
 'He keeps changing; <b>she stays calm</b>.')
P=dict(n=8,ch=3,out='/home/claude/work/devim/Devi_Mahatmya_Part08.html',
 title='Mahiṣāsura Slain · Part 8 · Devī Māhātmya series · srini.presents',
 desc='Devī Māhātmya, Part 8: chapter 3. The Goddess kills Mahiṣa’s generals, then Mahiṣāsura himself as he changes from buffalo to lion to man to elephant: “Roar, fool, roar while I drink.” Explained in plain English with illustrations.',
 h1='MAHIṢĀSURA SLAIN',series='DEVĪ MĀHĀTMYA · PART 8 · CHAPTER 3',tag='The buffalo, the lion, the man and the elephant · explained and illustrated',cover=cover,
 how='The story in <b>comic panels</b>, and the key ślokas of chapter 3 on their own pages: the chant, a picture, what it says, the key words, one insight and the one thing to remember.',
 where=[f'In {plink(7)}, the light of all the gods became the Goddess. Armed with every god’s weapon, she roared, and Mahiṣa’s armies fell like grass before fire.',
  'Chapter 3 is the duel. First the generals, then <b>Mahiṣāsura himself</b>, changing shape again and again. Its climax is the image of Durgā known everywhere: <b>Mahiṣāsuramardinī</b>, “she who crushes the buffalo demon”.'],
 wtalk=("Why is the villain a buffalo?","A buffalo is huge, stubborn and blind in its charge. That is tamas, the heavy dullness in all of us. Watch how she ends it."),
 fulls=[('THE KEY VERSES IN ONE GO','Chapter 3, ślokas 2–27',V[:5]),('IN ONE GO · CONTINUED','Chapter 3, ślokas 29–44',V[5:])],
 fullnote='Text as given in the Devīmāhātmyam edition of devimahatmyam.aurobharati.in (44 ślokas in chapter 3); its numbering is followed. Editions number them slightly differently.',
 seq=[st1]+V[:3]+[st2]+V[3:8]+V[8:],
 toc=[('THE GENERALS',[('generals','The Generals Fall')]+V[:3]),('THE SHAPE-SHIFTER',[('shapes','The Shape-Shifter')]+V[3:8]),('THE END OF MAHIṢA',V[8:])],
 end=['<b>The generals (3.1–20).</b> Cikṣura’s arrows are rain on a mountain; Cāmara falls to the lion; the other generals fall one by one.',
  '<b>The buffalo (3.21–27).</b> Mahiṣa scatters her troops; his rage breaks the earth, floods the ocean, tears the clouds.',
  '<b>The shapes (3.28–33).</b> Bound by her noose, he becomes a lion, a man, an elephant, a buffalo again.',
  '<b>The pause (3.34–38).</b> She drinks and laughs: “Roar, fool, roar for a moment, while I drink.”',
  '<b>The end (3.39–44).</b> Her foot on his neck, he comes half out of his mouth; she beheads him. Heaven sings and dances.'],
 endsub='Chapter 3 in five lines',
 next=("What's next?",'Part 9: chapter 4, the Śakrādi stuti. Indra and the gods sing one of the most beautiful hymns of the text: “She who is fortune in the homes of the good…”'),
 endkey='<b>however ego changes shape, she ends it</b>.',
 foot='Study notes on the Devī Māhātmya, chapter 3 (Mārkaṇḍeya Purāṇa). The story is retold in our words; ślokas are given as in the Devīmāhātmyam edition of devimahatmyam.aurobharati.in, whose numbering is followed. The reading of the buffalo as tamas and of the generals’ names is interpretive. Meanings are our own plain-English renderings. Zappy and Prof. Bolt are fictional.')
